  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nBreaks in the cloud cover have occurred over the region this\nafternoon, which has resulted in some surface heating through\nthe day. Current mesoanalysis shows ML CAPE around 500 j/kg\nacross northern OH and NW PA, ahead of an approaching cold\nfront. \n\nA line of showers and thunderstorms has developed along and\nahead of this front across southern Ontario through SE lower MI\nand NW OH. Extrapolation of this line indicates it should reach\nMercer county around 8 PM, and continue ESEwd thereafter through\nthe evening. Expect current instability levels to remain nearly\nsteady through the early evening hours, before quickly waning \nafter 9-10pm. 0-6km shear is expected to reach 40-50kt this\nevening in advance of the front. With the later arrival of the \nstorms, and diminishing instability later this evening, it \nappears there is a brief window for the potential for severe \nstorms, generally from 7-8 pm through 10-11pm. It still appears\nthat damaging wind is the main hazard from these storms, though\nlarge hail is also possible. The linear nature of the line\nshould preclude a more significant tornado threat, though\ndirectional shear indicates some rotation in embedded cells is\nnot out of the question. \n\nStorms should decay to sub-severe levels as they sink south \ntowards east-central OH/far southwest PA late this evening. \nRapid weakening should then continue, with the line nearly \ntotally dissipating as a few showers along the I-70 corridor \novernight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsembles remain in good agreement that large-scale \ntroughing remains in place over the Great Lakes/northeast CONUS\nthrough at least the end of the next work week, with near to \nbelow- normal temperatures throughout. A few shortwaves move \nthrough the larger trough through the period, providing shower \nchances. Minor strength/timing differences remain in the various\nsolutions.\n\nOne such wave completes passage on Monday, with surface high \npressure building in its wake Monday night, along with 850mb \ntemperatures dipping below zero. There is fairly high confidence in \nclear and calm conditions as the high center moves overhead. This \nwill represent our next (potentially last until fall?) chance at \nfrost/freeze conditions. NBM probabilities of minimum temperatures \n36 or below are 50 percent or greater to the north and east of \nPittsburgh, as well as in valley locations in the terrain. Headlines \nmay eventually be needed.",
